      "problem": "Given that  $({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1)={p}^{4}+a{p}^{3}+b{p}^{2}+cp+1,$ calculate $a+b+c.$",
      "worked_solution": "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}({p}^{2}+3p+1)+p({p}^{2}+3p+1)+1({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+3{p}^{3}+{p}^{2}+{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}+p+{p}^{2}+3p+1 & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+4{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2}+4p+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=4.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+4=13.\n$$",
